<!-- §01 THE QUADRATIC WALL -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Quadratic Wall</div>
<h2>Why Long Context Breaks Full Attention</h2>
<p>Every token in a Transformer must compute its attention over every preceding token. At sequence length <em>N</em>, this costs O(N²) floating-point operations — a quadratic wall that becomes catastrophic at long contexts.</p>
<p>At 1M tokens, a forward pass through standard Grouped-Query Attention spends 28× more FLOPs on attention than MiniMax Sparse Attention does — for the same model quality. That's the entire compute gap between "deployable" and "infeasible" at production scale.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">GQA vs MSA — Causal Attention FLOPs</div>
  <span class="math-var">F</span><sub>GQA</sub>(N) = 2 H<sub>q</sub> d<sub>h</sub> N² &nbsp; <span class="math-comment">→ quadratic in sequence length</span><br>
  <span class="math-var">F</span><sub>MSA</sub>(N) = H<sub>kv</sub> d<sub>idx</sub> N² + 4 H<sub>q</sub> d<sub>h</sub> N k B<sub>k</sub> &nbsp; <span class="math-comment">→ nearly linear when N ≫ kB<sub>k</sub></span><br><br>
  At N=1M: &nbsp; <span class="math-var">k</span>=16, <span class="math-var">B<sub>k</sub></span>=128 → kB<sub>k</sub>=2,048 selected tokens &nbsp; <span class="math-comment">→ 28.4× fewer FLOPs</span>
</div>

<p>The chart below shows the theoretical FLOPs divergence as context length grows. GQA (red) grows quadratically. MSA (purple) remains nearly flat because its main attention branch always touches only kB<sub>k</sub>=2,048 tokens — fixed regardless of context.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Attention FLOPs vs. Context Length — GQA vs. MSA</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">annotated at 1M</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding:.75rem 1.25rem 1.25rem">
    <canvas id="flops-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The key insight: MSA replaces the O(N²) main-branch cost with O(N·kB<sub>k</sub>) — where kB<sub>k</sub>=2,048 is a fixed budget. The remaining O(N²) term from the Index Branch uses a single shared head and a tiny projection dimension d<sub>idx</sub>, making it much cheaper than the full attention path.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 TWO-BRANCH DESIGN -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">02 — Two-Branch Design</div>
<h2>Index Branch + Main Branch</h2>
<p>MSA attaches a lightweight "Index Branch" to a standard GQA layer. The Index Branch acts as a scout: it cheaply scores every KV block and selects the top-k most relevant ones. The Main Branch then runs exact softmax attention — but only over the selected blocks.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">MSA Architecture — Two-Branch Design</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">KL alignment trains the scout</span>
  </div>
