<h3>The three training stabilizers</h3>
<p>Training a non-differentiable Top-k selector requires three tricks MSA borrows and combines:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>KL Alignment Loss</strong>: the Index Branch is trained to predict the same distribution as the Main Branch. This gives the scout a clear signal: "learn to select blocks that the main attention would focus on."</li>
  <li><strong>Gradient Detach</strong>: the KL loss is only allowed to update the scout's two projection matrices (W<sub>q</sub><sup>idx</sup>, W<sub>k</sub><sup>idx</sup>). Stop-gradient prevents it from modifying the model backbone — the backbone trains purely on language modeling loss.</li>
  <li><strong>Indexer Warmup</strong>: the first 40B tokens run full dense attention in both branches, training the scout until it can reliably predict what the main branch will attend to — only then does the main branch switch to sparse.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Key design choice:</strong> Each GQA group gets its own independent block selection. With 64 query heads and 4 KV heads, MSA maintains G=16 separate index sets — different groups can specialize in attending to different parts of the context.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §03 BLOCK SELECTION PATTERNS -->
<section id="s-selection" data-code="selection">
<div class="section-no">03 — Block Selection Patterns</div>
<h2>What the Model Learns to Select</h2>
<p>Once trained, the Index Branch learns a structured sparse pattern — not random sparsity, but a semantically meaningful one. The paper visualizes attention weights for four GQA groups across 32 KV blocks and finds a consistent three-part structure:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Local diagonal</strong> (cyan): the block containing the current query is always selected — the model retains perfect recall of its immediate neighborhood</li>
  <li><strong>Attention sink</strong> (amber): the very first block is always retrieved — a near-universal property of Transformers, where special tokens accumulate attention mass</li>
  <li><strong>Group-specific long-range</strong> (purple): each GQA group traces different "stripes" through the context — different groups specialize in attending to different temporal regions</li>
</ul>
<p>The heatmap below simulates this structure. Toggle between GQA groups to see how the long-range pattern changes while the local and sink columns remain constant.</p>

