<!-- §05 KERNEL ENGINEERING -->
<section id="s-kernels" data-code="kernels">
<div class="section-no">05 — GPU Kernel Engineering</div>
<h2>Making Sparse Fast: Four Key Tricks</h2>
<p>Sparse attention is theoretically fast but practically hard — irregular memory access patterns destroy GPU tensor-core utilization. MSA co-designs four kernel techniques to turn its theoretical 28× FLOPs reduction into real wall-clock speedup.</p>

<div class="step-list" id="kernel-steps">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Exp-free TopK selection</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Softmax is order-preserving: s<sub>i</sub> ≤ s<sub>j</sub> iff softmax(s)<sub>i</sub> ≤ softmax(s)<sub>j</sub>. The TopK indices are identical whether you sort raw scores or softmax probabilities. MSA bypasses the expensive max/exp/sum steps entirely and runs TopK directly on raw dot-product scores.</div>
      <div class="step-code">per-register min-heap · 32 lanes each hold k/32 elements · shuffle merge → TopK without softmax</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">KV-outer iteration (vs. Q-outer)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Standard FlashAttention iterates queries in the outer loop (arithmetic intensity ≈ G = 16). MSA iterates KV blocks in the outer loop, gathering all queries that selected each block. This packs up to ⌈128/G⌉ = 8 query positions × 16 heads into each 128×128 score MMA — arithmetic intensity ≈ ⅔ × B<sub>k</sub> ≈ 85. Five times higher utilization per operation.</div>
      <div class="step-code">FLOPs/IO: Q-outer ≈ G=16 · KV-outer ≈ ⅔×B_k=85 → better tensor-core util</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Pre-scheduled tile chunking</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Attention sinks create load imbalance: the first KV block is selected by nearly every query, giving one tile thousands of queries while others have tens. MSA pre-computes a chunk schedule that splits hot tiles across many CTAs. Each (query, chunk) pair is pre-assigned a slot in the output buffer — no atomic writes during attention, eliminating contention.</div>
      <div class="step-code">hot tile with 10,000 queries → split into ~5 chunks of 2,000 → 5× CTA fan-out</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Two-phase forward with LSE fusion</div>
      <div class="step-desc">KV-outer iteration splits each query's k attention partials across k CTAs — no single CTA can run inline softmax normalization. Phase 1 writes partial outputs with per-CTA log-sum-exp (LSE) values. Phase 2 reads all partials per query and combines them with rescaled weights. The KL-loss backward fuses LSE computation directly into the forward pass, eliminating a redundant kernel launch.</div>
      <div class="step-code">O = Σ_s exp(LSE_s - LSE_global) · O_buf[s] · exp(LSE_global - max LSE)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="kern-prev">← Prev</button>
  <button id="kern-next">Next →</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="kern-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
</div>

<h3>Benchmark: custom TopK vs. torch.topk</h3>
<p>At the deployed setting (k=16, B<sub>k</sub>=128, sequence length 128K), MSA's custom TopK kernel is <strong>5.1× faster</strong> than <code>torch.topk</code> and 3.7× faster than TileLang radix-select. This matters because TopK is on the critical path of every attention layer at every token position.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 RESULTS -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Benchmarks &amp; Speedup</div>
<h2>Matching Full Attention — With 14× Speedup</h2>
<p>MSA was validated on a 109B-parameter MoE model trained on 3T tokens — matching the scale of frontier models. Two variants are evaluated: MSA-PT (trained from scratch with sparse attention) and MSA-CPT (converted from a full-attention checkpoint via 400B continued pretraining).</p>

<p>The headline result: <strong>MSA-PT frequently outperforms full GQA attention</strong> on several benchmarks. This is counter-intuitive — sparse attention actually helps by forcing the model to develop better-organized representations that work under a fixed attention budget.</p>

